var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Writing-an-Extension","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"OutputVault's core stores nothing but what its extensions teach it. A package that has its own types to store, or a data format to store them in, extends three small interfaces. Everything below is tested against the core with a producer type of its own; OutputVaultTFIExt is the full example.","title":"Writing an Extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#The-producer-interface","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"A producer is a package with types to store. For each type it defines a storage kind, a namespaced string that is recorded in the file and selects the reader when the object is loaded:\n\nFunction Defined by the producer for its type Purpose\nOutputVault.storage_kind(x) \"MyPackage.Counter\" the kind recorded in the file; default nothing (the object is stored with JLD2 as it is)\nOutputVault.write_object(cfg, dir, name, x; provenance) write x into dir as name.<ext> the writer\nOutputVault.read_kind(Val(kind), ...) rebuild an object of that kind the reader\n\nThe core never names a producer's keys, and producers never name the core's: the producer writes through two helpers that record the kind, and the reader receives the file.","title":"The producer interface"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-type-stored-as-plain-data","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"using OutputVault, JLD2\n\nstruct Counter\n    counts::Vector{Int}\nend\n\nOutputVault.storage_kind(::Counter) = \"MyPackage.Counter\"\n\nfunction OutputVault.write_object(cfg::OutputVault.AbstractConfig, dir, name, x::Counter;\n                                  provenance = Dict{String,String}())\n    OutputVault.jld2_record(joinpath(dir, name * \".jld2\"), x) do io     # records the kind, then calls f(io)\n        io[\"counts\"] = x.counts\n    end\nend\n\nOutputVault.read_kind(::Val{Symbol(\"MyPackage.Counter\")}, path; model = nothing) =\n    Counter(JLD2.load(path, \"counts\"))\n\nprovenance holds strings that identify the run (the git commit id, for objects of the solution component); write them into the file if the object should carry them. model is what the caller of load supplied, for objects that store a model.\n\nNow the object works wherever the layout has a property for it:\n\nsave_result(rf, (; claims = Counter([1, 2, 3])))\nload(rf, :recursions).claims                          # Counter([1, 2, 3])","title":"A type stored as plain data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-type-stored-as-a-table","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"Tables go through the configuration's table format, so the producer needs no file format of its own:\n\nusing Tables, DataAPI\n\nstruct Series\n    t::Vector{Float64}\n    x::Vector{Float64}\nend\n\nTables.istable(::Type{Series}) = true\nTables.columnaccess(::Type{Series}) = true\nTables.columns(s::Series) = (t = s.t, x = s.x)\n\nOutputVault.storage_kind(::Series) = \"MyPackage.Series\"\n\nOutputVault.write_object(cfg::OutputVault.AbstractConfig, dir, name, s::Series; provenance = Dict{String,String}()) =\n    OutputVault.write_table(cfg, joinpath(dir, name), s)      # records the kind in the file's metadata\n\nOutputVault.read_kind(::Val{Symbol(\"MyPackage.Series\")}, source, path, fmt) =\n    Series(collect(Float64, Tables.getcolumn(source, :t)), collect(Float64, Tables.getcolumn(source, :x)))\n\nwrite_table writes the table with the metadata it publishes through DataAPI.jl (a type that defines DataAPI.metadata for its table has it stored) plus the storage kind. read_kind receives the table as read by the format, and its file-level metadata through DataAPI.metadata(source, key, default). An object saved as several tables, such as moments by sample, uses write_table_parts and gets back a NamedTuple of its parts in order.","title":"A type stored as a table"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Where-to-put-the-methods","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"Put them in a package extension of OutputVault, so that OutputVault stays light and the producer needs no dependency on OutputVault, or in the producer itself, which then depends on OutputVault. OutputVaultTFIExt is the first kind. In OutputVault's Project.toml:\n\n[weakdeps]\nMyPackage = \"uuid...\"\n\n[extensions]\nOutputVaultMyPackageExt = \"MyPackage\"\n\nwith the methods in ext/OutputVaultMyPackageExt.jl, in a module of that name. Choose storage kinds that start with the producer's package name, so that kinds of different producers never clash.","title":"Where to put the methods"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Model-metadata","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"A producer that stores solutions of models needs a way to rebuild the model from the file. Two functions are the hooks, with fallbacks for TFI's models in OutputVaultTFIExt:\n\nmodel_metadata(mdl) returns a NamedTuple of plain values (numbers, strings, symbols, numeric arrays) that describe the model; it is written into the solution file.\nmodel_from_metadata(::Type{M}, metadata) rebuilds a model of type M from it.\n\nOverload both for a model type whose fields are not all plain data.","title":"Model metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#A-new-table-format","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"Formats are the other axis: see Table Formats for the three functions and the package extension pattern (OutputVaultParquet2Ext is the model: eleven lines).","title":"A new table format"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#What-the-layout-does-not-do","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"The layout (LAYOUT) is fixed for every project: components and objects are not registered by extensions. A producer's types go in the existing objects. A genuinely new component or object is a change to OutputVault itself, which keeps every project's runs the same shape.","title":"What the layout does not do"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/extending/#Testing-an-extension","page":"Writing an Extension","text":"Test against a temporary output root and a throwaway git repository (the solution component records the commit id): save a result with dirty_repo_check = :ignore, load it, and compare. OutputVault's own tests do exactly this for a test-only producer type, in test/test_save_load.jl.","title":"Testing an extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#A-Research-Run,-Start-to-Finish","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"This page follows one model through everything OutputVault is for: solve and save, come back in a fresh Julia session, warm-start a nearby calibration, add a result computed later, and compare the runs. The model is the neoclassical growth model of TransitionFunctionIteration's quick start, so every step can be run as it is. Each block is one script, run in the process the text says.","title":"A Research Run, Start to Finish"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#Before-you-start","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"note: Load what you use\nOutputVault's core knows nothing about particular model packages or file formats. Each is a package extension, which activates when its packages are loaded in the session:using OutputVault\nusing TransitionFunctionIteration, StaticArrays, StructArrays   # TFI's solutions, simulations, moments, IRFs\nusing Parquet2                                                  # Parquet tables\nusing DuckDB, Tables                                            # assemble_moments, if you use it(StaticArrays and StructArrays are already loaded by TFI. If an extension is missing, the symptom is a method that does not exist or a message that names the package; see Troubleshooting.)\n\nwarning: Two things that are easy to get wrong\nSaving a solution replaces the run's folder. A save that includes obj or stv first deletes everything saved for that run: its simulation, statistics, IRFs, parameters and recursions are gone until you save them again. Re-running a solve to refresh the solution means re-saving the rest.The branch and the git record come from the working directory. RunHandle's default branch and the commit and diffs in solution/ are those of the git repository in the directory the process runs in, not of the output root or the model. Start Julia from the model's repository, or pass branch = ... to RunHandle explicitly; a reusable script should do both, and set its own Config.","title":"Before you start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#The-model","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"Saved as ngm.jl and included by every script below, because a restored solution needs its model type in the new process (the model's parameters are in the file, its code is not):\n\nusing TransitionFunctionIteration, StaticArrays\nimport TransitionFunctionIteration: Exogenv, calc_eqm, define_endog_grids, steadystate,\n                                     compute_next_period_values, bounds, PositiveBounded\n\n@kwdef struct NeoclassicalGrowthModel <: AbstractModel\n    α::Float64 = 0.33   # capital share\n    β::Float64 = 0.96   # discount factor\n    δ::Float64 = 0.10   # depreciation\n    γ::Float64 = 2.0    # CRRA risk aversion\n    σ::Float64 = 0.02   # shock volatility\n    ρ::Float64 = 0.9    # shock persistence\n    N_A::Int   = 5      # exogenous grid points\nend\n\nExogenv(mdl::NeoclassicalGrowthModel) =\n    ExogenvAR1(ρ = mdl.ρ, σ = mdl.σ, n = mdl.N_A, transform = exp, jensen = true, shock_name = :A)\n\nfunction steadystate(model::NeoclassicalGrowthModel, ::AnalyticalSteadyStateMode)\n    (; α, β, δ) = model\n    K = (α * β / (1 - β * (1 - δ)))^(1 / (1 - α))\n    C = K^α - δ * K\n    return SteadyState(; model, exog = (; A = 1.0), endog = (; K), values = (; C), guess = (; C)), true\nend\n\ndefine_endog_grids(model::NeoclassicalGrowthModel, stv::AbstractSteadyState) = (; K = range(0.8, 1.2, length = 20) * stv.endog.K)\n\nbounds(::SteadyState{NeoclassicalGrowthModel}) = (C = PositiveBounded(),), (K = PositiveBounded(),)\n\nfunction compute_next_period_values(next, mdl::NeoclassicalGrowthModel, ::EvaluationMode)\n    (; A, K, C) = next\n    (; α, β, δ, γ) = mdl\n    Kpayoff = α * A * K^(α - 1) + (1 - δ)\n    return NextPeriod(; expectations = (; Ek = β * C^(-γ) * Kpayoff), continuations = (; Kpayoff))\nend\n\nfunction calc_eqm(state, policy, mdl::NeoclassicalGrowthModel, np::NextPeriod, mode::EvaluationMode)\n    (; A, K) = state;  (; C) = policy;  (; α, δ, γ) = mdl\n    Y = A * K^α\n    fx = SVector(1 - np.expectations.Ek / C^(-γ))\n    mode isa SolveMode    && return fx\n    mode isa IterateMode  && return (; fx, trans = (; K = (1 - δ) * K + Y - C), vals = (; C))\n    return (; fx, trans = (; K = (1 - δ) * K + Y - C), simvars = (; Y, C))\nend","title":"The model"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#1.-Solve-and-save","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2, TransitionFunctionIteration\ninclude(\"ngm.jl\")\n\nroot = \"/path/to/outputs\"\nset_config!(Config(\n    output_root  = root,\n    table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat(),\n))\n\nmdl = NeoclassicalGrowthModel()\nobj, stv = main_setup(mdl)\nmain_execute!(obj; maxiter = 1000, logging = COMPLETE)\nsimseries, errs = simulate(obj, stv)\nstats    = describe(simseries).moments\nerrstats = calc_error_stats(errs)\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"demo\")              # branch: the current git branch\nsave_result(rf, (; obj, stv, stats, errstats, simseries, errs, params = collect_parameters(obj)))\n\nThe run is <root>/<branch>/res-demo-base/, with a folder per component (Runs and the Layout). Name runs deliberately: the experdef groups economies that belong together (a calibration, a version of the model) and must not contain -; the economy names the variant.","title":"1. Solve and save"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#2.-Reload-in-a-fresh-process","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"Start a new Julia session (a new day, another machine with the output folder) and load the portable copy:\n\nusing OutputVault, Parquet2, TransitionFunctionIteration\ninclude(\"ngm.jl\")                                   # the model type must exist in this process\n\nset_config!(Config(output_root = \"/path/to/outputs\",\n                   table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()))\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"demo\")\n\n(; obj, stv) = load(rf, :solution; solution_format = :portable, model = NeoclassicalGrowthModel)\n\n# the restored solution is the solution that was saved\nsaved = load(rf, :stats).stats.uncond\nsimseries, errs = simulate(obj, stv)\n@assert saved == describe(simseries).moments.uncond\n\nmodel = NeoclassicalGrowthModel rebuilds the model from the parameters in the file (NeoclassicalGrowthModel(; metadata...)); pass an instance instead to have it compared with the stored one. Treat the warning \"The supplied model's metadata differ from the stored model's\" as a question about reproducibility: the code or parameters are not those that produced the file. A model with derived or non-plain fields overloads model_metadata and model_from_metadata (TransitionFunctionIteration Objects). Do this check once for every model type: a fresh process, a portable load, and a simulation that matches.\n\nThe native copy (load(rf, :solution)) is faster but needs code compatible with the code that saved it, so use it only soon after the solve.","title":"2. Reload in a fresh process"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#3.-Warm-start-a-nearby-calibration","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"mdl2 = update_model(obj.model; ρ = 0.8)\nobj2, stv2 = main_setup(mdl2, obj)                  # starts from the reloaded solution\nmain_execute!(obj2; maxiter = 1000, logging = COMPLETE)\nsimseries2, errs2 = simulate(obj2, stv2)\n\nrf2 = RunHandle(\"lowrho\"; experdef = \"demo\")\nsave_result(rf2, (; obj = obj2, stv = stv2, stats = describe(simseries2).moments,\n                  simseries = simseries2, errs = errs2))\n\nEach economy is its own run, so the second save does not touch the first.","title":"3. Warm-start a nearby calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#4.-Add-a-result-computed-later","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"irf = simulate_irf(obj, simseries)\nsave_result(rf, (; irf))\n\nA save without obj or stv adds to the run: irf/ appears next to the other components, and solution/ is not touched. The same is how recursions and other derived results are saved when they are computed after the solution.","title":"4. Add a result computed later"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#5.-Compare-and-query","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"With the runs saved, compare them through TFI's moments (see its guide on moments):\n\nusing DuckDB, Tables, TransitionFunctionIteration\nusing TransitionFunctionIteration.Transformations: DiffPct, DiffLevel\n\nmoments = (std_K  = SimulationMoment(:K; stat = :std, compare_transformation = DiffPct()),\n           mean_C = SimulationMoment(:C),\n           irf_C  = ImpulseMoment(:C, :shock_5, 2; transformation = DiffLevel()))\n\nruns = find_matching_results(\"*/res-demo-*\")               # (base = RunHandle, lowrho = RunHandle)\ncompare_economies((; std_K = moments.std_K, mean_C = moments.mean_C), runs)   # each run's stats\n\ntable = assemble_moments(root, moments; experdef = \"demo\")  # one DuckDB query per kind of moment\ncompare_economies(moments, (:base,), table)\n\nassemble_moments reads only the statistics the moments name, and only from runs that have them. The lowrho run has no IRF, so it has no impulse rows in table, and the table has simulation rows for both. Asking for the impulse moment in lowrho is a KeyError that names the missing entry, which is how a result that was never computed shows up: as absent, never as zero. The same data are available to any language with DuckDB (Querying with DuckDB).","title":"5. Compare and query"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/lifecycle/#Troubleshooting","page":"A Research Run, Start to Finish","text":"What you see Cause Fix\nNo OutputVault config set. Call OutputVault.set_config!(cfg). no configuration in this process set_config!(Config(...)), or pass the config as the first argument\nsaving a ... needs a table format Config has no table_format Config(; table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat())\nParquetFormat needs its package: using Parquet2 the Parquet extension is not loaded using Parquet2 before using the format\nNo reader for kind TransitionFunctionIteration.…; load the package that stores it the file was written by a producer that is not loaded using TransitionFunctionIteration (and the package of any other kind named)\n... is a portable file without the model: load with model = ... a portable solution needs a model to rebuild load(...; solution_format = :portable, model = MyModel)\nThe supplied model's metadata differ from the stored model's the model's parameters or code are not those that saved the file find out what differs (the warning lists keys) before trusting the result\nno method matching assemble_moments(...) the DuckDB extension is not loaded using DuckDB, Tables, TransitionFunctionIteration\nEconomy folder ... does not exist a run that was never saved, or the wrong branch check RunHandle's branch: it defaults to the current git branch of the working directory\nexperdef \"...\" contains '-' the folder res-<experdef>-<economy> splits at the first hyphen no hyphens in experdef\nRepository is not clean. Saving git diffs to file. uncommitted changes in the working directory's repository expected; the diffs are saved next to the solution. dirty_repo_check = :ignore silences it","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Runs-and-configuration","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Runs and configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Saving-and-loading","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Saving and loading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Finding-runs","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Finding runs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Model-hooks","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Model hooks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Table-formats","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Table formats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-producer-interface","page":"API Reference","text":"For extensions that store the types of a package (Writing an Extension).","title":"The producer interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#DuckDB","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"DuckDB"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.RunHandle","page":"API Reference","text":"RunHandle(economy; branch=current_branch(), experdef=\"default\")\nRunHandle(; branch, experdef, economy)\n\nIdentifies a saved run by branch, experiment definition, and economy name.\n\nAll fields are coerced to String on construction. experdef must not contain -: the run's folder is res-<experdef>-<economy>, and readers (the DuckDB macros in outputvault.sql) split it at the first hyphen. economy may contain anything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.RunHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Config","page":"API Reference","text":"Config(; output_root, table_format=nothing, solution_format=(:portable, :native),\n         staging_root=joinpath(tempdir(), \"OutputVault\"))\n\nA project's OutputVault settings. Where each object goes is fixed (LAYOUT); which objects are saved is chosen in each save_result call.\n\nArguments\n\noutput_root: filesystem root for all saved outputs.\ntable_format: an AbstractTableFormat (e.g. OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()), needed to save tables (simulations, moments, IRFs).\nsolution_format: how the solution is saved: :portable (one file per object in a documented layout of plain arrays, readable without the code that wrote it), :native (all of the solution's objects in one JLD2 file, res.jld2, exactly as they are in Julia), or both. Other components are always portable.\nstaging_root: where save_result writes a replacing solution before moving it into place. If the move cannot be made (on Windows, files held open block it), the results stay here. Default: a folder in the system temp directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Config"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#OutputVault.LAYOUT","page":"API Reference","text":"LAYOUT\n\nWhere each saved property goes: one folder per component under the run's folder. Fixed, so that every project's runs have the same structure and tools can find objects across runs.\n\ncomponent properties\nsolution obj, stv\nstats stats (the moments compare_economies compares; moments by sample are saved one table per sample, stats/<sample>.<ext>)\nerrstats errstats\nsimulation simseries, errs\nirf irf\nparams params (the model's scalar parameters, see collect_parameters)\nrecursions claims, welfare (interpolants of recursions computed at a solution's policies; each is saved when it is computed, the solution is not touched)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.LAYOUT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.set_config!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_config!(cfg::AbstractConfig)\n\nStore cfg as the module-level OutputVault configuration. Returns cfg.\n\nCalled in a consuming package's __init__ so exported functions can be used without passing cfg explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.set_config!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.with_config","page":"API Reference","text":"with_config(f, cfg::AbstractConfig)\n\nExecute f() with cfg as the active configuration, then restore the previous config. The previous config is restored even if f throws. Useful in tests and multi-project REPL sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.with_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.output_path","page":"API Reference","text":"output_path(cfg::AbstractConfig, branch=current_branch()) -> String\noutput_path(branch=current_branch()) -> String\n\nReturn the branch-level output directory output_root(cfg)/branch, creating it if absent. The no-config form uses the module-level config set by set_config!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.output_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.save_result","page":"API Reference","text":"save_result(cfg::AbstractConfig, rf::RunHandle, result; dirty_repo_check) -> String\nsave_result(rf::RunHandle, result; kwargs...) -> String\n\nSave the properties of result that LAYOUT names, each in its component's folder under the economy folder for rf, one file per property. Which objects are saved is the caller's choice: properties that result lacks or that are nothing are not saved; properties outside the layout are ignored. Returns the folder holding the results.\n\nThe economy folder holds the results of one solution: a save that includes solution objects (obj, stv) replaces the folder, so that no results of an earlier solution remain. The new results are written to staging_root(cfg) first, then the old folder is renamed aside, the new one moved into place and the old one deleted. If a step fails (on Windows, files held open, such as ones a load memory-mapped, block renames and deletes), nothing is lost or half-deleted: a warning gives the paths, the old results stay at the economy folder, and the return value is the staging folder holding the new results. Saves without solution objects (e.g. IRFs computed later) add to the economy folder.\n\nThe solution is saved in the formats solution_format(cfg): :portable writes one file per object (as every other component), :native writes all its objects to res.jld2 with JLD2, exactly as they are. The solution folder also receives the git commit id (git_commit.txt) and, for a dirty repository, the staged and unstaged diffs (git_staged.diff, git_unstaged.diff; the latter includes untracked files). Solution objects get the commit id as provenance.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ndirty_repo_check: :warn (default), :error, or :ignore. Controls behavior when the repository has uncommitted changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.save_result"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#FileIO.load","page":"API Reference","text":"load(cfg::AbstractConfig, rf::RunHandle, component::Symbol; solution_format = :native, model = nothing) -> NamedTuple\nload(rf::RunHandle, component::Symbol; kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nLoad a saved component (see LAYOUT) and return a NamedTuple of the properties that were saved, in layout order. Objects come back as their own types (e.g. a TFI ModelSolution or SimulationPanel). The no-config form uses the module-level config.\n\nFor the solution, solution_format picks the copy that is read: :native (res.jld2, the objects exactly as they were saved; needs compatible code) or :portable (one file per object; objects that store a model, such as TFI solutions and steady states, need model, either a model or a model type, see load_solution). Other components are always portable and ignore both keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FileIO.load"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.save_solution","page":"API Reference","text":"save_solution(path, obj; provenance = Dict{String,String}())\n\nWrite a model solution to a single portable HDF5-compatible file of plain data. Methods come with package extensions (TransitionFunctionIteration: grids, exogenous environment, the values of Pfct, Vfct, Tfct and Ffct at the grid nodes, the bounds, and metadata, including the model's). provenance holds strings identifying the run, e.g. a git commit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.save_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.load_solution","page":"API Reference","text":"load_solution(path, mdl)                 # supplied model, compared with the stored metadata\nload_solution(path, M::Type)             # model rebuilt by model_from_metadata(M, metadata)\n\nRestore a solution written by save_solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.load_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.save_steadystate","page":"API Reference","text":"save_steadystate(path, stv)\n\nWrite a steady state to a portable file of plain data, with the model's metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.save_steadystate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.load_steadystate","page":"API Reference","text":"load_steadystate(path, mdl)              # supplied model, compared with the stored metadata\nload_steadystate(path, M::Type)          # model rebuilt by model_from_metadata(M, metadata)\n\nRestore a steady state written by save_steadystate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.load_steadystate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.find_matching_results","page":"API Reference","text":"find_matching_results(cfg::AbstractConfig, wildcard=\"$(current_branch())/res-*\"; key_format=:auto)\nfind_matching_results(wildcard=...; kwargs...)\n\nGlob the output root for folders matching wildcard, parse their names as res-{experdef}-{economy}, and return a NamedTuple of RunHandles.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nkey_format: :auto (default), :economy, :experdef_economy, or :branch_experdef_economy. :auto selects the shortest format that yields unique keys.\n\nThrows ArgumentError if the chosen format produces duplicate keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.find_matching_results"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.get_results_folders","page":"API Reference","text":"get_results_folders(cfg::AbstractConfig, economies; experdef=nothing, branch=nothing)\nget_results_folders(economies; kwargs...)\n\nConstruct a NamedTuple of RunHandles keyed by economy name for each entry in economies (a single name or a collection). experdef and branch default to the RunHandle constructor defaults when not supplied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.get_results_folders"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.model_metadata","page":"API Reference","text":"model_metadata(mdl) -> NamedTuple\n\nPlain-data description of a model: reals, strings, symbols, small numeric arrays and NamedTuples of these. Stored with portable solutions and steady states, and compared with a supplied model when one is loaded. Methods for TransitionFunctionIteration models come with its extension; overload it for models whose fields include derived objects, together with model_from_metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.model_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.model_from_metadata","page":"API Reference","text":"model_from_metadata(::Type{M}, metadata::NamedTuple) -> M\n\nRebuild a model of type M from model_metadata output (used by load_solution(path, M)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.model_from_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.collect_parameters","page":"API Reference","text":"collect_parameters(obj_or_model) -> MomentArray\n\nThe model's parameters as a table to save in the params component: one row per parameter, one column value. The default for TFI models (in the TransitionFunctionIteration extension) takes every scalar real or Boolean field of the model (a Boolean is 0 or 1); a project can add a method for its model type to choose differently. Save it with save_result(rf, (; params = collect_parameters(obj))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.collect_parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.AbstractTableFormat","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractTableFormat\n\nHow tables (simulations, moments, IRFs) are stored. A format implements table_extension(fmt), write_table_file(fmt, path, table, meta) (write the table with its own metadata plus the file-level entries meta, a Dict{String,String}) and read_table_file(fmt, path) (a Tables.jl source whose file metadata DataAPI.metadata reads). Built-in formats are in OutputVault.Formats, with their methods in package extensions; TableFormat takes the functions directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.AbstractTableFormat"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats","page":"API Reference","text":"Built-in table formats. Each needs its IO package loaded (using Parquet2 for ParquetFormat), which loads the package extension that implements it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat","page":"API Reference","text":"ParquetFormat()\n\nParquet files written and read with Parquet2 (needs using Parquet2). Keeps file metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.TableFormat","page":"API Reference","text":"TableFormat(; ext, write, read)\n\nA table format from its functions: the file extension, write(path, table, meta) and read(path), with the contract of AbstractTableFormat. For formats without a built-in type in OutputVault.Formats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.TableFormat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.table_extension","page":"API Reference","text":"table_extension(fmt), write_table_file(fmt, path, table, meta), read_table_file(fmt, path)\n\nThe table-format contract (see AbstractTableFormat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.table_extension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.storage_kind","page":"API Reference","text":"storage_kind(x) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nThe kind OutputVault records with x, which selects the read_kind method that restores it. Producers define it for their types, namespaced by package (e.g. \"TransitionFunctionIteration.ModelSolution\"). Default nothing: stored with JLD2 as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.storage_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.write_object","page":"API Reference","text":"write_object(cfg, dir, name, x; provenance)\n\nWrite x into dir as name.<ext>. Producers add methods for their types, built on write_table or jld2_record so that the kind is recorded; other objects are stored with JLD2 as name.jld2. provenance holds strings identifying the run (the git commit id, for the git component).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.write_object"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.write_table","page":"API Reference","text":"write_table(cfg, stem, table; meta = Dict{String,String}())\n\nWrite a Tables.jl table as stem.<ext> with the config's table format, recording storage_kind(table) and the entries meta in the file's metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.write_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.write_table_parts","page":"API Reference","text":"write_table_parts(cfg, dir, name, parts::NamedTuple)\n\nWrite object name, a NamedTuple of tables, as one table per part, dir/<part>.<ext>. Each file records the object it belongs to and its position, so that loading returns the NamedTuple with its parts in order. Producers use it for objects such as moments by sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.write_table_parts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.jld2_record","page":"API Reference","text":"jld2_record(f, path, x) -> path\n\nCreate the JLD2 file path, record storage_kind(x) in it, and call f(io) to write the rest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.jld2_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.recorded_kind","page":"API Reference","text":"recorded_kind(path) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nThe storage kind recorded in a JLD2 file, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.recorded_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.read_kind","page":"API Reference","text":"read_kind(::Val{kind}, path; model)\nread_kind(::Val{kind}, source, path, fmt)\n\nRestore an object of the recorded kind from a JLD2 file, or from a table source read from path with table format fmt. Producers add methods for their kinds. model is what the caller of load supplied (nothing, a model or a model type), for objects that store a model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.read_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.duckdb_sql_path","page":"API Reference","text":"duckdb_sql_path() -> String\n\nPath of outputvault.sql, the DuckDB table macros over an output root (vault_stats, vault_errstats, vault_simulation, vault_runs). Load it into a DuckDB connection and query, e.g. SELECT * FROM vault_stats('<output_root>').\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.duckdb_sql_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.duckdb_statements","page":"API Reference","text":"duckdb_statements() -> Vector{String}\n\nThe statements of outputvault.sql, comments removed, for clients that run one statement per call. With DuckDB.jl: foreach(s -> DBInterface.execute(con, s), duckdb_statements()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.duckdb_statements"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.assemble_moments","page":"API Reference","text":"assemble_moments(root, moments; branch = \"**\", experdef = \"*\", economies = nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nRead the statistics that moments (a NamedTuple of TransitionFunctionIteration moments) need from the saved runs under the output root root, in one DuckDB query for the simulation moments (vault_stats) and one for the impulse moments (vault_irf). Returns a column table with one row per (economy, statistic): branch, experdef, economy, kind, group, variable, stat, period, value, the long form that TFI's MomentTable and compare_economies(moments, names, table) read.\n\nbranch and experdef are glob patterns that narrow the files read (only matching runs are opened); economies is an optional list of economy names to keep. Only runs that have the component are read: an economy without saved statistics (or IRFs, for impulse moments) is simply absent from the result, and the table is empty if there are none. Needs DuckDB and TransitionFunctionIteration loaded (it is implemented in a package extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.assemble_moments"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Saving-and-Loading","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"","title":"Saving and Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Configuration","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"A Config holds a project's settings:\n\nusing OutputVault, Parquet2\n\ncfg = Config(\n    output_root     = \"/path/to/outputs\",\n    table_format    = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat(),\n    solution_format = (:portable, :native),     # the default; or :portable, or :native\n)\n\noutput_root: where all runs are saved.\ntable_format: how tables (simulations, moments, IRFs) are stored; see Table Formats. Without one, saving a table is an error that says so.\nsolution_format: which copies of the solution to save (Runs and the Layout).\nstaging_root: where a replacing solution is written before it is moved into place (default: a folder in the system temp directory); see Replacing a run.\n\nEvery function that needs a configuration takes one as its first argument, and has a form without it that uses the active configuration. A package or script sets it once:\n\nset_config!(cfg)                      # e.g. in a package's __init__\nwith_config(cfg2) do                  # scoped: the previous one is restored, even on an error\n    save_result(rf, result)\nend\n\nFor libraries that need more than the data in Config, a subtype of OutputVault.AbstractConfig implements output_root, and optionally table_format and solution_format.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Saving","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"rf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\")\nsave_result(rf, (; obj, stv, stats, errstats, simseries, errs, irf, params))   # returns the run's folder\n\nsave_result saves the properties of the result that LAYOUT names. A property that the result lacks, or that is nothing, is not saved, and properties outside the layout are ignored, so one call can be as large or as small as you like:\n\nsave_result(rf, (; obj, stv, stats))               # without the simulation\nsave_result(rf, (; irf))                           # IRFs computed later: added to the folder\nsave_result(rf, (; claims))                        # a recursion computed later\n\nA save with obj or stv replaces the run's folder (Runs and the Layout). The keyword dirty_repo_check (:warn, the default; :error; or :ignore) controls what happens when the repository has uncommitted changes.","title":"Saving"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Replacing-a-run","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"A save with obj or stv writes the new results to staging_root first. It then renames the old run folder to .replaced-<folder>-<time> next to it, moves the staged folder into place and deletes the renamed one. On Windows, a file held open blocks renames and deletes of its folder, and Parquet2 keeps the files it has read memory-mapped until Julia's garbage collector has run, so a load followed closely by a save can hit this. Nothing is lost or half-deleted when it does:\n\nIf the old folder cannot be renamed, it stays intact at the run's path, the new results stay in staging_root, a warning gives both paths, and save_result returns the staging path.\nIf only the final delete fails, the new results are in place and a warning names the leftover .replaced-* folder, which discovery and DuckDB ignore. Delete it by hand.\n\nThe cure is to release whatever holds the files (close handles, leave the folder as the working directory, let the garbage collector run) and move the staged folder into place.","title":"Replacing a run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Loading","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"load(rf, :stats)           # (stats = ...,)       the properties that were saved, in layout order\nload(rf, :solution)        # (obj = ..., stv = ...)  the native copy\nload(rf, :recursions)      # (claims = ..., welfare = ...) whichever exist\n\nload returns a NamedTuple of the saved properties of a component, each restored as its own type. It is FileIO.load extended; OutputVault re-exports it. For the solution:\n\nload(rf, :solution; solution_format = :portable, model = MyModel)    # the portable copy\n\nA portable solution stores the model's parameters as plain data. To rebuild the Julia object, model is either a model instance, which is compared with the stored metadata (a warning lists what differs), or a model type, which is rebuilt from the metadata with model_from_metadata. Single files can be read directly with load_solution(path, model) and load_steadystate(path, model). Moment tables saved by sample load as a NamedTuple with the samples in the order they were saved.","title":"Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Finding-runs","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"get_results_folders([\"base\", \"alt\"]; experdef = \"v1\")   # (base = RunHandle, alt = RunHandle)\nfind_matching_results(\"main/res-v1-*\")           # glob the output root, parse the folder names\n\nfind_matching_results returns a NamedTuple of RunHandles keyed by the shortest combination of names that is unique (economy, then experdef_economy, then branch_experdef_economy), or by the key_format you pass. Folders whose names do not parse as res-<experdef>-<economy> are ignored.","title":"Finding runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Where-things-are","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"output_path()                       # <output_root>/<current branch>, created if absent\nOutputVault.economy_folder(rf)      # <output_root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<economy>","title":"Where things are"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Table-Formats","page":"Table Formats","text":"Tables (simulations, moment tables, IRFs) are stored through the configuration's table format, so that the choice of file format belongs to the project and not to the code that produces a table. A table is any Tables.jl source, written together with its metadata: string key-value pairs that say what the table is and where it came from. TFI's types restore as their own types from the metadata, so a format must keep file-level metadata for them.","title":"Table Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Parquet","page":"Table Formats","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2\nConfig(; output_root = \"...\", table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat())\n\nParquetFormat writes and reads Parquet files with Parquet2 (.parquet). Loading Parquet2 loads the extension that implements it; without it, using the format fails with a message that names the package to load. Parquet stores the metadata as file-level key-value pairs, and DuckDB, pandas and Arrow read the files directly.\n\nThe keys in a file written by OutputVault:\n\nKey Meaning\noutputvault.kind the storage kind, e.g. TransitionFunctionIteration.MomentArray, which selects the reader\noutputvault.part_of, outputvault.part_index for an object saved as several tables (moments by sample): the object and the position\ntfi.* the producer's own (TFI's kind, row key, endogenous states, provenance)","title":"Parquet"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Another-format","page":"Table Formats","text":"A format implements three functions for its type:\n\nusing OutputVault\nimport OutputVault: AbstractTableFormat, table_extension, write_table_file, read_table_file, required_package\n\nstruct MyFormat <: AbstractTableFormat end\n\ntable_extension(::MyFormat) = \"myext\"\nrequired_package(::MyFormat) = \"MyIOPackage\"            # named in the error if its methods are missing\n\n# write `table` (a Tables.jl source) to `path`, with its own metadata plus the entries of\n# `meta::Dict{String,String}` as file-level metadata\nwrite_table_file(::MyFormat, path, table, meta) = MyIOPackage.write(path, table; metadata = meta)\n\n# a Tables.jl source whose file-level metadata DataAPI.metadata(source, key, default) reads\nread_table_file(::MyFormat, path) = MyIOPackage.read(path)\n\nPut the methods in a package extension of OutputVault triggered by the IO package, as OutputVaultParquet2Ext is, and a user selects the format with Config(; table_format = MyFormat()) after loading the IO package. For a quick format without a package extension:\n\nfmt = TableFormat(ext = \"myext\",\n                  write = (path, table, meta) -> ...,\n                  read  = path -> ...)\n\nA format without file-level metadata (plain CSV) can store tables but cannot restore TFI's types from them, because the kind is recorded in the metadata.","title":"Another format"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Quick-Start","page":"Quick Start","text":"This page needs no model package. It saves and loads a run with a tiny stand-in for a producer: a table type of three lines of methods, so that you see what OutputVault does and what a package that stores its own types has to provide (Writing an Extension). For the same lifecycle with a real model, see A Research Run, Start to Finish.\n\nRun it from inside a git repository: the solution folder records the repository's commit.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#A-producer","page":"Quick Start","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2, Tables\n\n# summary statistics as a table: variable, mean, std\nstruct Summary\n    variable::Vector{String}\n    mean::Vector{Float64}\n    std::Vector{Float64}\nend\nTables.istable(::Type{Summary}) = true\nTables.columnaccess(::Type{Summary}) = true\nTables.columns(s::Summary) = (variable = s.variable, mean = s.mean, std = s.std)\n\nOutputVault.storage_kind(::Summary) = \"Demo.Summary\"\nOutputVault.write_object(cfg::OutputVault.AbstractConfig, dir, name, s::Summary; provenance = Dict{String,String}()) =\n    OutputVault.write_table(cfg, joinpath(dir, name), s)\nOutputVault.read_kind(::Val{Symbol(\"Demo.Summary\")}, source, path, fmt) =\n    Summary(string.(Tables.getcolumn(source, :variable)),\n            collect(Float64, Tables.getcolumn(source, :mean)), collect(Float64, Tables.getcolumn(source, :std)))\n\nThe kind \"Demo.Summary\" is written into the file and selects the reader when the object is loaded. Objects without a kind are stored with JLD2 as they are.","title":"A producer"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Configure-and-save","page":"Quick Start","text":"root = \"/path/to/outputs\"\nset_config!(Config(output_root = root, table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()))\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\")                  # branch: the current git branch\n\nsave_result(rf, (; obj = (α = 0.33, β = 0.96),           # the \"solution\": any object\n                   stats = Summary([\"K\", \"C\"], [3.5, 1.2], [0.4, 0.1])))\nreaddir(OutputVault.economy_folder(rf))                  # [\"solution\", \"stats\"]\n\nThe run is <root>/<branch>/res-v1-base/. obj and stats are two of the objects the layout knows (Runs and the Layout); the table went through the configuration's table format, as a Parquet file.","title":"Configure and save"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Add-a-result-later","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_result(rf, (; irf = Summary([\"K\", \"C\"], [0.0, 0.01], [0.0, 0.0])))\nreaddir(OutputVault.economy_folder(rf))                  # [\"irf\", \"solution\", \"stats\"]\n\nA save without a solution adds to the run and leaves the solution alone.","title":"Add a result later"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Load","page":"Quick Start","text":"load(rf, :stats).stats.mean                              # [3.5, 1.2]\nload(rf, :solution).obj                                  # (α = 0.33, β = 0.96)\n\nload returns a NamedTuple of the objects that were saved in a component, each restored as its own type.","title":"Load"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Saving-a-solution-replaces-the-run","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_result(rf, (; obj = (α = 0.33, β = 0.95)))\nreaddir(OutputVault.economy_folder(rf))                  # [\"solution\"]\n\nA save that includes obj or stv deletes everything saved for the run first, so that nothing in the folder was computed from another solution. The statistics and the late result are gone: save them again.","title":"Saving a solution replaces the run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Query-every-run-with-DuckDB","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_result(rf, (; stats = Summary([\"K\", \"C\"], [3.4, 1.19], [0.4, 0.1])))\n\nusing DuckDB\ncon = DBInterface.connect(DuckDB.DB)\nforeach(s -> DBInterface.execute(con, s), duckdb_statements())     # load the macros\nDBInterface.execute(con, \"SELECT economy, variable, stat, value FROM vault_stats('$root') ORDER BY variable, stat\")\n\nvault_stats reads the stats tables of all runs under the root in long form, from any language with DuckDB (Querying with DuckDB); a table with a variable column and one column per statistic is what it expects.","title":"Query every run with DuckDB"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#TransitionFunctionIteration-Objects","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"OutputVaultTFIExt stores the types of TransitionFunctionIteration.jl. It loads when TransitionFunctionIteration, StaticArrays and StructArrays are loaded. TFI itself writes no files: it defines Tables.jl interfaces and metadata for its result types, and this extension writes them with the project's table format.","title":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#What-is-stored-where","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"TFI type Object File Format\nModelSolution solution/obj obj.jld2 (and res.jld2) plain arrays and metadata (JLD2)\nSteadyState solution/stv stv.jld2 plain arrays and metadata (JLD2)\ndescribe(...).moments stats/stats <sample>.<ext>, one per sample table\nMomentArray (error statistics) errstats/errstats errstats.<ext> table\nSimulationPanel, SimulationErrorPanel simulation/simseries, simulation/errs simseries.<ext>, errs.<ext> table\nIRFResult irf/irf irf.<ext> and irf_paths.<ext> two tables\nMomentArray (parameters) params/params params.<ext> table\nInterpolant recursions/claims, recursions/welfare <name>.jld2 plain arrays (JLD2)\n\nEvery object records its storage kind (for example TransitionFunctionIteration.SimulationPanel) in its file, and load restores the object as that type.","title":"What is stored where"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#Solutions-and-steady-states","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"The portable solution file holds plain HDF5-compatible arrays and metadata, with no Julia objects, so it reads without the code that wrote it, in Python with h5py for example. Files are written uncompressed because h5py cannot read JLD2's compressed datasets. Layout, schema version 2:\n\noutputvault/kind                         storage kind\nmeta/{schema_version, tfi_version, created, solution_id, model_type, singletons}\nmeta/model/...                           the model's metadata (model_metadata)\nmeta/provenance/<key>                    strings from the caller (the git commit id)\ngrid/<axis>, grid/__axes                 grid vectors (a singleton axis has length 1) and their order\nexogenv/points/<var>, .../__names        the exogenous states' values and their order\nexogenv/mtrans, exogenv/ssidx            transition matrix and steady-state index\nPfct/<var>, Vfct/<var>, Ffct/<var>       arrays over the grid axes (Pfct in unconstrained space)\nTfct/<var>                               arrays over the grid axes and next period's exogenous state\n<fct>/__vars                             variable order\nPfct/__bounds/<var>, Tfct/__bounds/<var> [lower, upper], plus __names\n\nA steady-state file has meta/..., the model's metadata, and guess/, endog/, exog/, values/ (one entry per name plus __names). It does not store the model: a steady state takes its model from the solution file in the same folder.\n\nThe schema version is an integer that changes only when a change breaks readers of earlier files; the reader accepts only the current version, and added entries do not change it.","title":"Solutions and steady states"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#Rebuilding-the-model","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"A portable solution stores the model's metadata, the model's fields as plain data: model_metadata(mdl) keeps reals, strings, symbols and numeric arrays and tuples with at most 1000 elements, recursively through structs and named tuples to depth 4, and drops everything else (functions, interpolants, large arrays). Loading needs a way back to a model:\n\nload(rf, :solution; solution_format = :portable, model = mdl)         # an instance: checked against the metadata\nload(rf, :solution; solution_format = :portable, model = MyModel)     # a type: rebuilt from the metadata\n\nThe default rebuild is MyModel(; metadata...). A model package whose models need more (derived objects that were dropped, nested parameter structs) overloads model_metadata and model_from_metadata for its type. The solution id (solution_id in TFI), a hash of the grid and the values of the functions, is stored and checked on load, and a mismatch warns that the file may be corrupted.","title":"Rebuilding the model"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#Tables","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"TFI's tables carry their own metadata (tfi.kind, tfi.rowkey, tfi.endog, tfi.provenance), and OutputVault adds its storage kind (outputvault.kind); both are in the file's metadata, so TFI's constructors restore the right type. TFI's tables restore as their own types only from table formats that keep file-level metadata (Parquet does).\n\nA MomentArray is written with the row names in the column named by its row key: variable for moments, equation for error statistics, parameter for parameters. Moments by sample (describe(...).moments) are one file per sample, in the order they were saved, which is recorded in each file's metadata so that load returns them in order.","title":"Tables"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#Parameters-and-recursions","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"save_result(rf, (; params = collect_parameters(obj)))\n\ncollect_parameters turns the model's scalar parameters (every real or Boolean field, a Boolean as 0 or 1) into a one-column MomentArray with row key parameter, which is what the params component holds and what a DuckDB query returns as vault_params. A model package can define its own method to choose differently.\n\nThe recursions component holds interpolants over the solution's grid, one file each, for quantities computed at the solution's policies after the solve. The file holds its own grid, so it loads without the solution, and saving one never touches the solution folder.","title":"Parameters and recursions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tfi/#Comparing-saved-runs","page":"TransitionFunctionIteration Objects","text":"using TransitionFunctionIteration\ncompare_economies(moments, (base = RunHandle(\"base\"), alt = RunHandle(\"alt\")))\n\ncompares moments (see TFI's guide on moments) across runs read with the active configuration: each run's stats, and its IRFs if a moment is an ImpulseMoment. For queries over many runs, assemble_moments reads only the statistics that the moments need, in one DuckDB query (Querying with DuckDB).","title":"Comparing saved runs"},{"category":"section","location":"#OutputVault.jl","page":"Home","text":"Research models produce a lot of output: solutions, simulations, moments, impulse responses. Across git branches, experiment definitions and variants of the model, it is easy to lose track of which code produced which numbers. OutputVault.jl gives every run one place on disk, the same place for every project, and records the code version next to the results.\n\nA fixed layout. <root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<economy>/<component>/<object>.<ext>. Tools find an object in any run without knowing the project; see Runs and the Layout.\nProvenance. The solution folder holds the git commit and, for a dirty repository, the diffs. Saving a new solution replaces the run's folder, so a folder never mixes results of two solutions.\nPlain files. Solutions are plain arrays and metadata in HDF5-compatible files, tables are Parquet. A reader in Python, R, MATLAB or DuckDB needs no Julia code.\nExtensions. The core knows nothing about particular model packages or file formats. Each is a package extension that loads when its package does:\n\nExtension Loads with Provides\nOutputVaultTFIExt TransitionFunctionIteration, StaticArrays, StructArrays storage of TFI's solutions, steady states, simulations, moments, IRFs and interpolants; model parameters\nOutputVaultParquet2Ext Parquet2 Formats.ParquetFormat: tables as Parquet files with file metadata\nOutputVaultDuckDBExt DuckDB, Tables, TransitionFunctionIteration assemble_moments and compare_economies over saved runs\n\nOther solution packages and table formats extend the same interfaces; see Writing an Extension.","title":"OutputVault.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2\n\nset_config!(Config(\n    output_root  = \"/path/to/outputs\",\n    table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat(),     # tables need a format\n))\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\")                     # branch: the current git branch\nsave_result(rf, (; obj, stats))                             # run inside a git repository\nsave_result(rf, (; irf))                                    # later: adds to the run\nload(rf, :stats).stats                                      # NamedTuple of the saved objects\n\nThe Quick Start page runs exactly this, with a tiny table type and no model package, and queries the saved runs with DuckDB. With TransitionFunctionIteration, the result of solve_and_simulate is already in the right shape:\n\nresult = solve_and_simulate(mdl)\nsave_result(rf, merge(result, (; params = collect_parameters(result.obj))))\n\nFrom any language with DuckDB, for example Python:\n\nimport duckdb\ncon = duckdb.connect()\ncon.execute(open(\"outputvault.sql\").read())    # the macros; the path is OutputVault.duckdb_sql_path()\ncon.sql(\"SELECT * FROM vault_stats('/path/to/outputs', experdef_glob := 'v1')\")","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"git@github.com:elenev/OutputVault.jl.git\")\n\nThe package requires Julia 1.9 or later. Add the packages of the extensions you use (Parquet2 for tables, TransitionFunctionIteration for its types, DuckDB for queries).","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Quick Start: saving, adding to, loading and querying a run, with no model package.\nA Research Run, Start to Finish: one model through solve, save, a fresh-process reload, a warm start, a late result and a comparison, with the things that go wrong.\nRuns and the Layout: what a run is and where each object goes.\nSaving and Loading: configuration, save_result, load, git provenance, finding runs.\nTransitionFunctionIteration Objects: what is stored for TFI's types, and the file layouts.\nTable Formats: how tables are stored and how to add a format.\nQuerying with DuckDB: the SQL macros and assemble_moments.\nWriting an Extension: storing the types of another package.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Runs-and-the-Layout","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"","title":"Runs and the Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#What-a-run-is","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"A run is the saved results of one economy: one solved model with its simulation and statistics. It is identified by three names, held in a RunHandle:\n\nField Meaning Default\nbranch the git branch of the code that produced it the current branch of the working directory\nexperdef the experiment definition: a named set of economies (calibration, version of the model) \"default\"\neconomy the economy within the experiment, e.g. base or alt required\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\")      # branch: current_branch()\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\", branch = \"feature-x\")\n\nA run lives in a folder of the project's output root:\n\n<output_root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<economy>/\n\nexperdef must not contain -, because readers split the folder name at the first hyphen after res-; economy may contain anything (including hyphens). A branch with a / (feature/x) is a nested folder. The names can be anything else: OutputVault imposes no convention on economy names, and the DuckDB macros can split them into parts with a regular expression (Querying with DuckDB).","title":"What a run is"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Components-and-objects","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"Inside the run folder, results are grouped into components, one folder each, with one file per object (property). Where each object goes is fixed for every project and every user of OutputVault, so that tools can find an object in any run. LAYOUT is the table:\n\nComponent Objects Holds\nsolution obj, stv the solved model and its steady state; the git commit and diffs\nstats stats moments of the simulation; one table per sample, stats/<sample>.<ext>\nerrstats errstats equation-error statistics\nsimulation simseries, errs the simulated panel and its equation errors\nirf irf impulse responses (statistics and raw paths)\nparams params the model's scalar parameters\nrecursions claims, welfare interpolants computed at the solution's policies\n\nres-v1-base/\n├── solution/     obj.jld2  stv.jld2  res.jld2  git_commit.txt  [git_staged.diff  git_unstaged.diff]\n├── stats/        uncond.parquet\n├── errstats/     errstats.parquet\n├── simulation/   simseries.parquet  errs.parquet\n├── irf/          irf.parquet  irf_paths.parquet\n├── params/       params.parquet\n└── recursions/   claims.jld2  welfare.jld2\n\nWhich objects a run has is the caller's choice: each save_result saves the objects it is given, so a run can be built in steps (the recursions are often computed after the simulation).","title":"Components and objects"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#One-folder,-one-solution","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"The solution component is special. A save that includes obj or stv first deletes the whole run folder, then writes the new solution, so everything in a run folder was computed from the solution in it. Saves without solution objects add to the folder, and replace an object saved before. The reasons are practical: stale diffs and simulations next to a new solution are the usual way to mislead yourself about which results belong to which code.","title":"One folder, one solution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Provenance","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"The solution folder records the code:\n\ngit_commit.txt: the commit id of the repository in the working directory;\nif the repository is dirty, git_staged.diff and git_unstaged.diff (the latter including untracked files), unless a diff exceeds 1 MB. The diffs are computed without touching the repository's index.\n\nsave_result warns about a dirty repository by default, or fails with dirty_repo_check = :error. TFI's simulations and IRFs record in their own metadata the id of the solution they were computed from, so a simulation can be traced to its solution.","title":"Provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Two-copies-of-the-solution","page":"Runs and the Layout","text":"The solution is saved twice by default (solution_format = (:portable, :native)):\n\nportable: one file per object (obj.jld2, stv.jld2) in a documented layout of plain arrays and metadata, readable without the code that wrote it. This is the copy for long-term storage, for other languages and for loading with a rebuilt model (TransitionFunctionIteration Objects).\nnative: res.jld2, the objects exactly as they are in Julia. Quick to load, but it needs code compatible with the code that wrote it, so it is meant for work done soon after the solve.\n\nEvery other component is always portable.","title":"Two copies of the solution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#Querying-with-DuckDB","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"Saved runs are plain Parquet files in a fixed layout, which DuckDB can query in place, from any language it has a client for. OutputVault ships the SQL that does it: sql/outputvault.sql, whose path is duckdb_sql_path(). It defines table macros that take the output root and return the contents of all runs under it in long form. OutputVault does not depend on DuckDB.","title":"Querying with DuckDB"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#Loading-the-macros","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"Python (or the DuckDB command line):\n\nimport duckdb\ncon = duckdb.connect()\ncon.execute(open(\"outputvault.sql\").read())\ncon.sql(\"SELECT * FROM vault_stats('/path/to/outputs') WHERE variable = 'K' AND stat = 'mean'\")\n\nDuckDB.jl runs one statement per call; duckdb_statements() returns them:\n\nusing DuckDB, OutputVault\ncon = DBInterface.connect(DuckDB.DB)\nforeach(s -> DBInterface.execute(con, s), duckdb_statements())\nDBInterface.execute(con, \"SELECT * FROM vault_runs('/path/to/outputs')\")\n\nStatements in the file are separated by ; at the end of a line, so clients that split on that work too. The file is the same on every machine: the root is an argument.","title":"Loading the macros"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#The-macros","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"Each takes the output root first and returns long-form data, with the run identity as columns:\n\nMacro Columns after branch, experdef, economy\nvault_runs(root) (none: the saved runs)\nvault_stats(root) sample, variable, stat, value\nvault_errstats(root) equation, stat, value\nvault_params(root) parameter, value\nvault_irf(root) shock, stat, period, variable, value\nvault_simulation(root) the simulation panel's columns (__run, __period, the variables)\n\nvalue is a DOUBLE. For IRFs, period counts from 1, the starting period before the shock; the baseline shock is uncond.\n\nThe column holding a moment table's row names is the row key of the MomentArray that wrote it (variable, equation or parameter), which is why the macros differ in their second column.","title":"The macros"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#Reading-only-what-you-need","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"The root is usually a synced folder with online-only files, and a glob over every run would open them all. Every macro takes named arguments that narrow the files that are read:\n\nSELECT * FROM vault_stats('/root', branch_glob := 'main', experdef_glob := 'v1');\nSELECT * FROM vault_stats('/root', economies := ['base', 'alt']);\n\nbranch_glob (default '**') and experdef_glob (default '*'): glob patterns on the folder names.\neconomies (default ['*']), for the macros over per-economy components: a list of economy glob patterns. Each pattern must match a saved component of some run, or DuckDB reports that no files match.","title":"Reading only what you need"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#Splitting-the-economy-name","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"OutputVault imposes no convention on economy names. To split them into columns, pass all five arguments, the last two a regular expression with one capture group per part and the parts' names:\n\nSELECT * FROM vault_stats('/root', '**', '*', '^([^_]+)(?:_(.+))?$', ['model', 'scheme'])\nWHERE variable = 'K' AND stat = 'mean';\n\nadds the columns model and scheme after economy, empty where a group does not match.","title":"Splitting the economy name"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/duckdb/#Moments-from-saved-runs:-assemble_moments","page":"Querying with DuckDB","text":"With DuckDB, Tables and TransitionFunctionIteration loaded, assemble_moments reads exactly the statistics that a set of TFI moments need, from the saved runs, in one query per kind of moment:\n\nusing OutputVault, DuckDB, Tables, TransitionFunctionIteration\nusing TransitionFunctionIteration.Transformations: DiffLevel\n\nmoments = (std_K = SimulationMoment(:K; stat = :std),\n           irf_C = ImpulseMoment(:C, :up, 2; transformation = DiffLevel()))\n\ntable = assemble_moments(root, moments; branch = \"main\", experdef = \"v1\",\n                         economies = [:base, :alt])\n\nThe result is a column table with one row per (economy, statistic): branch, experdef, economy, kind, group, variable, stat, period, value. It is what TFI's MomentTable reads, so\n\ncompare_economies(moments, (:base, :alt), table)                       # a MomentArray\ncompare_economies(moments, (:base, :alt), root; branch = \"main\", experdef = \"v1\")   # both steps\n\nOnly runs that have the component are queried: an economy without saved statistics, or without IRFs for an impulse moment, is simply absent from the table, and a moment that cannot be read in an economy that is present is a KeyError that names it.","title":"Moments from saved runs: assemble_moments"}]
}
